  "final_text": "I finished all four steps and checked each result by reading it back from Kibana.\n\n1. **Data view 'Kibana Sample Data Flights'** (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has `timestamp` (a date) as its time field. Its other fields:\n   - **Numbers:** `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, `DistanceMiles`, `FlightDelayMin`, `FlightTimeMin`, `dayOfWeek`, `hour_of_day`\n   - **Yes/no fields:** `Cancelled`, `FlightDelay`\n   - **Text values:** `Carrier`, `FlightNum`, `FlightDelayType`, `FlightTimeHour`, plus origin and destination details (`Origin`/`Dest`, `\u2026AirportID`, `\u2026CityName`, `\u2026Country`, `\u2026Region`, `\u2026Weather`)\n   - **Map locations:** `OriginLocation`, `DestLocation`\n   - Kibana's standard fields: `_id`, `_index`, `_score`, `_source`, `_ignored`\n\n2. **Dashboard 'S4-8892c5ef Flights'** (id `7985bee3-5603-4bf4-ab6e-e69e0e3ea9cf`) has exactly two panels, both bar charts on that data view:\n   - number of flights over time, using `timestamp`\n   - number of flights for each of the top 10 carriers, using `Carrier`\n\n3. **Alert rule 'S4-8892c5ef Cancelled flights'** (id `9e588449-446b-4df1-94d0-cdc93a09c8e4`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute, counts flights matching `Cancelled : true` over the last 5 minutes by `timestamp`, and fires when the count is above 10. It was enabled when created, so I didn't need to turn it on.\n\n4. **Edit and check:** the carrier panel's title is now 'Flights by carrier', and the dashboard still has exactly two panels. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`, a 1-minute schedule, a threshold of more than 10 over 5 minutes, and a last run status of `ok`.\n\nThe rule counts flights by their `timestamp`, which is the only time field the data has, so \"arrive\" here means the flight's recorded time.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
